s. So to understand how constrained that effective freedom really is, we need to look at the massive empirical finding that kicked this whole philosophical inquiry off in the first place. Right. Because Fliction's paper is essentially a really deep theoretical analysis of another foundational empirical study. A study by a team of researchers, Zhang et al. And I mean, what Zhang and their team did is frankly kind of mind-blowing to me. It really is an incredible experiment. Zhang et al. decided to test a very simple yet radical hypothesis. So they took a fully pre-trained transformer model, right? Yes. And just so we're all on the same page here. Pre-trained means this AI has already read like a massive chunk of the entire internet. Right. And it's already spent months just absorbing text. Yeah. It knows grammar. It knows historical facts. It knows how to predict the next word in a sentence perfectly. It is fully functional. It has a deeply established internal organization. That's the key. So they took this highly capable pre-trained model and they subjected it to reinforcement learning or RL. Okay. Reinforcement learning. Let's define that really quick. That is where we stop just having it passively read text and we start giving it a specific goal, right? Exactly. Like solve this complex math problem. And we give it a mathematical reward when it gets the right answer. Kind of like giving a dog a treat when it does a trick. That's a perfect analogy. Yeah. You are actively trying to teach it a new specific capability that it didn't have before. Right. Now, usually when you do RL, you let the algorithm adjust all the parameters in the model. You let the whole digital brain adapt to the reward signal. You use your nominal freedom. Right. But Zhang et al didn't do that. Instead, they froze almost everything. When you say froze, you mean they literally locked the math down. Literally locked it down. The entire network, every single parameter was completely rigid except f