8, "text": " Right. The things you have access to. Yeah. Let's say you have a massive language model with a hundred", "tokens": [51220, 1779, 13, 440, 721, 291, 362, 2105, 281, 13, 865, 13, 961, 311, 584, 291, 362, 257, 5994, 2856, 2316, 365, 257, 3262, 51452], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056741364505312857, "compression_ratio": 1.7733812949640289, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809647207169376e-12}, {"id": 28, "seek": 13844, "start": 160.18, "end": 166.1, "text": " layers. You technically have the freedom to tweak the parameters in every single one of those hundred", "tokens": [51452, 7914, 13, 509, 12120, 362, 264, 5645, 281, 29879, 264, 9834, 294, 633, 2167, 472, 295, 729, 3262, 51748], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.056741364505312857, "compression_ratio": 1.7733812949640289, "no_speech_prob": 1.6809647207169376e-12}, {"id": 29, "seek": 16610, "start": 166.1, "end": 171.14, "text": " layers. That is your nominal space. Exactly. You have the keys to the entire building. You can walk", "tokens": [50365, 7914, 13, 663, 307, 428, 41641, 1901, 13, 7587, 13, 509, 362, 264, 9317, 281, 264, 2302, 2390, 13, 509, 393, 1792, 50617], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.03654471858517154, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779777878989875e-12}, {"id": 30, "seek": 16610, "start": 171.14, "end": 177.29999999999998, "text": " into any room. But the effective freedom is an entirely different concept. Because effective freedom", "tokens": [50617, 666, 604, 1808, 13, 583, 264, 4942, 5645, 307, 364, 7696, 819, 3410, 13, 1436, 4942, 5645, 50925], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.03654471858517154, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779777878989875e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 16610, "start": 177.29999999999998, "end": 183.18, "text": " represents the parts you can actually change without, well, breaking the things the system already knows", "tokens": [50925, 8855, 264, 3166, 291, 393, 767, 1319, 1553, 11, 731, 11, 7697,